  The first-order column divided by q, and the second-order column
  divided by q^2, should each be constant down a site block. Constancy
  in the second column confirms the expansion is perturbative at 1 e and
  that every result here transfers to smaller charges by scaling.
  Departure indicates higher-order or charge-transfer contamination,
  which Sokalski (1985) notes has exponential rather than polynomial
  character and would invalidate the parity separation at this magnitude.

